WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:29.720
ai just spotted a brand new biological system hidden inside viruses that could one day help rewrite medicine. This is Models &amp; Agents for Beginners, Episode 176. You know that feeling when you find a hidden room in a game you thought you already finished? A research team at Anthropics said Claude, their ai model, located a new enzyme system in bacterial phage DNA. Anthropic is the company that built Claude. A model here just means the trained

00:00:29.720 --> 00:00:34.140
program you chat with, like a study buddy that has read an enormous library.

00:00:34.880 --> 00:00:38.780
An enzyme is a tiny helper molecule that does one specific job.

00:00:39.380 --> 00:00:43.780
Think of it as one specialized tool in a huge toolbox, not the whole workshop.

00:00:44.720 --> 00:00:49.120
DNA is the instruction code inside living things, written in chemical letters.

00:00:50.280 --> 00:00:53.520
Bacteriophages are viruses whose only targets are bacteria.

00:00:53.520 --> 00:01:00.380
picture them as microscopic robots that ignore plants, animals, and people, and chase bacteria alone.

00:01:01.120 --> 00:01:06.920
Claude scanned huge amounts of genetic data. It flagged a strange repeating pattern next to a gene

00:01:06.920 --> 00:01:13.160
that looked similar to CRISPR, the famous gene editing tool. Gene editing means changing those

00:01:13.160 --> 00:01:19.680
DNA instructions, almost like fixing one sentence in a long essay. This new system appears to cut,

00:01:19.680 --> 00:01:25.420
that, copy, and paste DNA, matching a few other rare natural tools scientists already know.

00:01:25.920 --> 00:01:30.500
It is a bit like finding extra buttons on a keyboard that can snip and rearrange letters.

00:01:31.560 --> 00:01:35.000
Discoveries like this have historically led to big medical breakthroughs.

00:01:35.420 --> 00:01:38.840
CRISPR itself turned into the foundation for new genetic medicines.

00:01:39.560 --> 00:01:43.880
Researchers are wondering whether this unknown system could eventually do something similar.

00:01:44.520 --> 00:01:45.820
The team is being careful.

00:01:45.820 --> 00:01:50.460
They still need many more experiments in the lab to learn exactly what the system does

00:01:50.880 --> 00:01:52.700
and whether it can be used safely.

00:01:53.420 --> 00:01:58.260
Claude is acting like a super fast research assistant that can spot biology patterns people

00:01:58.260 --> 00:01:58.900
might miss.

00:01:59.420 --> 00:02:00.860
It does not replace scientists.

00:02:01.480 --> 00:02:06.540
It speeds up the early idea stage so people in lab codes can test the most promising leads.

00:02:07.200 --> 00:02:09.640
You cannot run those biology tests at home.

00:02:10.120 --> 00:02:13.600
You can try the same kind of thinking on Anthropics Claude website.

00:02:13.600 --> 00:02:13.700
Right.

00:02:14.080 --> 00:02:19.460
Ask it to brainstorm ideas for a school science project, or ask it to explain a biology

00:02:19.460 --> 00:02:23.960
concept in simple words, and watch how it connects ideas you might have skipped.

00:02:24.620 --> 00:02:26.120
Here is how the search works.

00:02:26.480 --> 00:02:31.460
When you hunt for a song on your phone, a few words can pull up the right track among millions.

00:02:32.180 --> 00:02:36.060
Now swap songs for billions of letters of DNA code from viruses.

00:02:36.720 --> 00:02:40.600
The AI does not read every letter one by one the way a person would.

00:02:40.600 --> 00:02:44.120
It has learned patterns from huge amounts of training data.

00:02:44.600 --> 00:02:49.780
Training data is the pile of examples it studied beforehand, like a mountain of flashcards

00:02:49.780 --> 00:02:54.660
before a test, so it can quickly flag when something looks unusual or important.

00:02:55.360 --> 00:02:58.360
Next it shows the strange pattern to human biologists.

00:02:58.980 --> 00:03:02.720
Those biologists decide whether it is worth testing in a real lab.

00:03:03.300 --> 00:03:08.520
The AI is doing the first round of interesting or not filtering at a speed no human team

00:03:08.520 --> 00:03:09.160
could match.

00:03:09.160 --> 00:03:14.340
Think of a teacher skimming a thousand essays and pulling only the surprising ones for

00:03:14.340 --> 00:03:15.160
a closer look.

00:03:15.740 --> 00:03:17.160
This is not random guessing.

00:03:17.620 --> 00:03:21.840
It is the same kind of pattern recognition you use when you recognize a friend's face

00:03:21.840 --> 00:03:22.480
in a crowd.

00:03:23.180 --> 00:03:28.160
Only instead of faces, it is recognizing shapes and repeats inside genetic code.

00:03:28.940 --> 00:03:33.520
Scientists then get a short list of promising ideas instead of checking everything themselves.

00:03:34.360 --> 00:03:36.680
That is why the discovery happens so fast.

00:03:36.680 --> 00:03:42.100
First, once you see it as pattern spotting at giant scale, the speed starts to make sense.

00:03:42.680 --> 00:03:48.880
A professor named Ethan Mollick told an AI called Opus to make fully animated movie-style

00:03:48.880 --> 00:03:52.140
Skyrim loading screens of whatever the AI liked best.

00:03:52.860 --> 00:03:58.060
Skyrim is a famous fantasy video game, and loading screens are the art you stare at while

00:03:58.060 --> 00:03:59.080
a level boots up.

00:03:59.580 --> 00:04:04.940
Opus is another chat model you type a request to, the way you might text a very fast artist.

00:04:04.940 --> 00:04:10.120
The result is a looping site full of moving scenes that feel like they belong in the game.

00:04:10.740 --> 00:04:16.180
Search for Elder Favorite Skyrim. Open the site on your phone or laptop and scroll through

00:04:16.180 --> 00:04:22.620
the animated screens. Notice how the AI kept the classic Skyrim art style while swapping in its

00:04:22.620 --> 00:04:29.500
own favorite objects. Malik also asked Claude to rebuild rescue raiders, a game he loved as a kid.

00:04:29.500 --> 00:04:34.180
The rebuild came with updated graphics, new goals, and tech trees.

00:04:34.760 --> 00:04:40.620
A tech tree is a map of upgrades you unlock as you play, like a skill path in a modern game.

00:04:41.520 --> 00:04:46.620
The AI worked with helper agents to keep improving the game until it felt fun and playable.

00:04:47.340 --> 00:04:54.640
Helper agents are extra AI teammates that each take a piece of the job, like a group project where every person has a role.

00:04:54.640 --> 00:04:59.780
search for rescue raiders, open the rebuilt game, and try playing a few levels.

00:05:00.400 --> 00:05:05.360
Pay attention to how the graphics and gameplay feel compared with the original 1980s version.

00:05:06.320 --> 00:05:10.800
Super Forecasters are people who are unusually good at predicting future events.

00:05:11.460 --> 00:05:18.540
Last year the best of them gave AI only a 1.7% chance of solving a major unsolved math problem by now.

00:05:19.280 --> 00:05:21.920
The actual progress turned out to be much faster.

00:05:21.920 --> 00:05:27.440
A new graph shows the price of reaching good scores on tough math and science tests is

00:05:27.440 --> 00:05:30.460
falling quickly, even as the AI gets smarter.

00:05:31.180 --> 00:05:35.780
It is a bit like calculators getting cheaper and better at the same time, until they fit

00:05:35.780 --> 00:05:36.660
in every backpack.

00:05:37.480 --> 00:05:42.240
Cheaper AI means more people and schools can use powerful tools without spending a lot.

